0205-0242 definition def:architecture-axis-map [Architecture-axis map]
The compiler architecture uses five disjoint evidence axes. An accepted claim may read evidence from an axis only at the admissible evidence level shown in the displayed axis map; the negative boundary in the last column is part of the claim certificate and cannot be discharged by prose from another axis. center tabularx{}{p{.16}p{.27}p{.27}X} Axis & Role in this paper & Admissible evidence & Negative boundary \\ BEDC source theory & finite-kernel theory, proof obligations, and implementation contracts & pinned source paths, commits, imported labels, bounded summaries, and source-interface records & not a publication acceptance record, not a fresh rebuild, and not a complete BEDC exposition \\ Lean markers & formal-verification records for named obligations & recorded build, axiom-purity, marker-existence, or source-interface rows when named by the package & not object-language closure, not discovery by itself, and not proof of unrelated claims \\ Finite witnesses & manifest/evaluator substrate evidence for artifacts and package certificates & manifest rows, evaluator outcomes, bundled snapshots, verifier logs, file digests, and limitation ledgers & not semantic universality, not w ...

0244-0258 proposition prop:architecture-axis-non-escalation [Axis non-escalation]
In any record-gate-sound package using Definition~def:architecture-axis-map, passage or evidence on one architecture axis cannot certify a load-bearing claim whose claim kind requires another axis. More precisely, if a claim certificate uses an axis $A$ but its stronger reading needs an evidence item or negative-boundary discharge listed only on a different axis $A'$, then the stronger reading is not accepted unless the package contains a separate submitted coordinate for $A'$ and the gates assigned to that coordinate pass. Consequently Theorem~thm:publication-safety-interface constructs only finite publication certificates whose axis coordinates are explicitly present in the submitted support surface; it does not infer BEDC closure from Lean markers, artifact semantics from manifest rows, proof evidence from agents, venue upload from automath route state, or support-surface availability from local bundle paths.

