Let $r$ be any accepted manuscript claim whose discovery component cites one of the imported labels in Table~tab:source-interface-record. If $r$ uses only Lemma~lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness, then the imported-label premise of $r$ is accepted only at the manual path-verified bounded-paraphrase level. If $r$ additionally cites Proposition~prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency, then the same premise is upgraded only by the finite record comparisons listed in that proposition: table--JSON equality, manifest agreement, snapshot digest and line count agreement, and label containment in the declared ranges. In neither case does the premise become a fresh Lean/BEDC rebuild, a proof of the semantic truth of the imported source theorem, or an unbounded restatement of the source text. Consequently the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~thm:publication-safety-interface, can use the imported discovery-gate spine only through these two explicitly separated evidence levels.

0590-0752 definition def:operational-acceptance-interface [Operational acceptance interface]
A claim is a manuscript assertion that can affect a mathematical, artifact, route, or submission decision. A load-bearing claim is a claim whose removal would change a theorem statement, a case-study conclusion, or the submission-readiness status. An advisory output is any AI-agent draft, score, suggested theorem, review packet, or repair proposal before a gate records it. A deterministic gate is a repeatable predicate, script, or checklist whose inputs and pass/fail decision are recorded independently of the prose generated by an agent. A human promotion is the explicit editorial act that moves a candidate into publication state, for example by creating an active 2026\_* paper directory with main.tex and PIPELINE.md. An evidence surface is the set of source paths, commits, commands, logs, inventories, and witness records cited for a claim. An acceptance entry is a manuscript or submission-ledger record that marks a candidate claim as usable in the publication-facing argument. Accepted work is the subset of candidate claims with such an entry. This is a status notion only: the definition records that an editorial acceptance entry exists, but it does not by itself assert external ed ...

0754-0776 definition def:stronger-reading-preorder [Stronger-reading preorder]
Fix the dispatch table $$ and certificate tuple $ C(c)=(K,,E,H,R,D,B)$ of Definition~def:operational-acceptance-interface. A proposed reading $c^{+}$ of the same prose claim is record-stronger than $c$, written $c_{} c^{+}$, if at least one of the following finite coordinates is strictly enlarged while the others are unchanged or enlarged: the claim-kind set $K$; the evidence level, from $path$ to $command$; the evidence surface $E$; the promotion field $H$; the assigned gate-pass family $R$; the discovery-interface field $D$; or the boundary field $B$. It is properly stronger if the enlargement is strict in at least one coordinate. It is accepted as a stronger reading only when the enlarged tuple still satisfies $Acc_{}$: every newly added kind has its assigned gates, every newly raised evidence level has the command tuple required by A6, and every new evidence item is on the submitted support surface or in an explicit historical boundary. The preorder is intentionally syntactic and finite-record based. It is not a semantic entailment relation between mathematical propositions, and it does not turn an inventory entry, digest row, or path reference into proof evidence for a theorem ...

