Under the hypotheses of the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~thm:publication-safety-interface, the publication-safety conclusion is invariant under replacement of the surrounding implementation by any other implementation with the same submitted acceptance entries, evidence surfaces, promotion fields, discovery-interface rows, and evidence-level boundaries. Therefore the theorem cannot be used, without an additional submitted record-gate-soundness certificate, to infer external workflow correctness, current execution of unrecorded gates, Lean correctness, artifact semantic validity, or venue acceptance. Conversely, any manuscript assertion that tries to promote the finite certificate constructor to one of those stronger implementation claims has a finite overclaim witness: either the required field is absent from \( C(c)\), or the row's declared evidence level exceeds the cited certificate surface.

